Basic Git Commands
Creating a New Branch
---------------------

# Create a new branch (stays on current branch)
git branch <branch-name>

# Create and switch to new branch in one command
git checkout -b <branch-name>

# Modern way (Git 2.23+)
git switch -c <branch-name>


Switching Branches
---------------------

# Switch to an existing branch
git checkout <branch-name>

# Modern way (Git 2.23+)
git switch <branch-name>

# Switch back to previous branch
git checkout -


Making Commits
---------------------

# Check status of changes
git status

# Add specific file to staging
git add <filename>

# Add all files to staging
git add .
# or
git add -A

# Commit with message
git commit -m "Your commit message here"

# Add and commit in one command (only for already tracked files)
git commit -am "Your commit message here"


Viewing Branches
---------------------

# List local branches
git branch

# List all branches (including remote)
git branch -a

# See branch with last commit
git branch -v


Merging Branches
---------------------

# First switch to target branch (e.g., main)
git checkout main

# Then merge other branch into current branch
git merge <branch-name>

Deleting Branches
---------------------

# Delete local branch (after merging)
git branch -d <branch-name>

# Force delete local branch
git branch -D <branch-name>


Pushing to Remote
---------------------

# Push branch to remote
git push origin <branch-name>

# Push and set upstream
git push -u origin <branch-name>

Pulling Updates
---------------------

# Pull latest changes from remote
git pull

# Fetch changes without merging
git fetch


Common Workflow Example
---------------------

# Create and switch to new feature branch
git checkout -b feature/new-feature

# Make changes, then stage and commit
git add .
git commit -m "Add new feature"

# Switch back to main branch
git checkout main

# Merge feature branch
git merge feature/new-feature

# Delete feature branch (optional)
git branch -d feature/new-feature